Within the Pythia family, 1.4B shows seed dependence: s42 yields only 2/8 (the sole outlier across all conditions), while s43 recovers to 6/8.
Qwen3.5-0.8B (PermG~fwd~8/8) extends the finding beyond the Pythia/GPT-2/OPT decoder-only family to an independently trained architecture with a different tokenizer.

\paragraph{Domain generalization.}
Replacing WikiText-103 with C4 yields the strongest detection: Pythia-1B achieves PermG~fwd~8/8 in both seeds, confirming that the lead-lag pattern is not an artifact of narrow topical distribution.

\paragraph{Canary metric sensitivity: the SmolLM3-3B case.}
\label{sec:smollm3}
SmolLM3-3B (PermG fwd 3/8) reveals that canary metric choice is itself a dimension of method sensitivity.
Decomposing by canary type: ECE$\to$downstream achieves 3/4 significant pairs, while token entropy$\to$downstream achieves 0/4.
The root cause is that SmolLM3's token entropy exhibits non-monotonic oscillations across generations, violating the monotonic decline pattern that Granger tests exploit; ECE, by contrast, maintains its monotonic upward trajectory.
Multi-method consensus confirms the severity: entropy pairs activate only via naive and differenced cross-correlation (per-pair $K{=}2$), ECE pairs activate only via differenced cross-correlation and permutation Granger (per-pair $K{=}2$), and no pair exceeds two-method agreement---illustrating that even within a single architecture, the method panel fragments along canary metric boundaries.

\begin{abstract}
We show that lead-lag conclusions in iterative model collapse are fragile: method choice alone inflates spurious-signal rates to at least 62.5\% (inter-method $\kappa{=}0.091$--$0.185$), with the most liberal method yielding 75\% forward-direction FPR even at the null ($\alpha{=}0.0$).
We identify three pitfalls (method sensitivity, spurious regression under shared trends, and regime dependence) and propose the Multi-Method Consensus Test (MMCT), a weighted consensus framework with null-calibrated $p$-values that achieves FPR${=}$0\% with detection rate increasing monotonically from $\alpha{=}0.25$ through $\alpha{=}0.75$.\footnote{MMCT detection rates pending unified cross-script audit; permutation Granger results are unaffected.}
Cross-architecture validation across 14 model-seed combinations confirms permutation Granger detection (${\geq}6/8$ metric pairs) in 12 cases (86\%), with multi-method consensus ($K{\geq}3 \geq 6/8$) in 10 of 14 configurations.
First-differencing cleanly separates genuine from spurious channels: all canary$\to$diversity leads survive while all canary$\to$perplexity leads vanish.
Applied to Pythia-410M and replicated across six model families (345M--6.9B parameters), the framework identifies token entropy as a one-generation-ahead canary for diversity collapse: permutation Granger forward significance reaches ${\geq}6/8$ in 12 of 14 cross-architecture conditions at full synthetic replacement, with directional asymmetry (24/24 forward, 0/24 reverse) confirmed across four seeds.
\end{abstract}

\paragraph{Regime boundary uncertainty.}
The onset boundary at $\alpha{\approx}0.45$--$0.50$ has been refined by probing at $\alpha{=}0.35$, $0.40$, and $0.45$ (all 0/8), narrowing the transition to a ${\leq}0.05$-wide interval; a faint single-pair signal appears at $\alpha{=}0.25$ (FDR $p{=}0.053$) but does not reach aggregate detection.
Whether this boundary is sharp (phase-transition-like) or gradual, and whether it shifts with model scale or data properties, remains open.
Three-seed replication at $\alpha{=}0.75$ confirms directional asymmetry (20/24 forward, 0/24 reverse) but method consensus remains transitional (Tier~2--3), leaving the exact boundary position uncertain.
The canary blind spot observed at $\alpha{=}0.10$ (diversity collapsed but canary silent) rests on a single seed; multi-seed confirmation is needed to establish whether this dissociation is robust.

\paragraph{No intervention experiment.}
We observe that token entropy Granger-precedes diversity collapse but do not test whether acting on canary signals (e.g., halting training or reducing $\alpha$) can prevent or delay collapse onset.
Without intervention evidence, the practical value of the canary remains theoretical.

\paragraph{Replace-only protocol.}
Our experiments use replace-only data accumulation; accumulate-mode training~\cite{dey2024breaking,kazdan2025collapse} may exhibit qualitatively different canary dynamics or eliminate the need for canary monitoring entirely~\cite{falahati2026curated}.

\paragraph{Scope limited to diversity metrics.}
Our canary claims are scoped to distinct-$n$ diversity metrics~\cite{li2016diversity}, not to model collapse broadly~\cite{schaeffer2025position}, as the perplexity channel proved spurious after differencing.
This scoping is theoretically motivated by the calibration-diversity tradeoff~\cite{cao2025entropycal}, but means our findings may not generalize to other collapse manifestations (e.g., factual degradation, reasoning capability loss).
